{"id":"h117","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"claim","question":"Is the following claim true? The chemical formula of table salt is KCl.","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"author-written for this project; verified by the author","claims":[{"text":"Table salt is an ionic chloride compound.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"KCl (potassium chloride) is a real compound sold as a salt substitute.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1414},{"text":"KCl could plausibly be confused with table salt.","confidence":0.6,"lean":0.2,"theta":0.9425,"phi":-3.1414},{"text":"Table salt is sodium chloride, NaCl.","confidence":0.98,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"NaCl is the standard chemical identity of table salt in kitchens and chemistry references.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.9,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"KCl is a different compound from NaCl, with different properties and a different cation.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1415},{"text":"The identity of table salt is a matter of definition and common chemical fact.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The claim that table salt is KCl names the wrong compound and is false.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Chemical similarity and the salt-substitute connection do not change what table salt is.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.7,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":5,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":5,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":4,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":5,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":6,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":7,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":8,"J":0.4,"relation":"supp